            "name": "Overview",
            "value": "<div data-page-id=\"YP3XdinBZozo9Qx3V4elcqv1gBd\" data-lark-html-role=\"root\" data-docx-has-block-data=\"false\">\n<div class=\"ace-line ace-line old-record-id-HDITdoYgwoY086xiwLNlV7m0gJe\">Overall, there was an upward trend in wind energy production in all four nations throughout the period shown. In addition, the United States and Denmark were the leading producers, with their figures experiencing the most substantial growth.</div>\n</div>",
            "question_id": 13784,
            "is_toggle_on": true
          },
          {
            "id": 389,
            "sort": 3,
            "name": "Body 1",
            "value": "<div data-page-id=\"YP3XdinBZozo9Qx3V4elcqv1gBd\" data-lark-html-role=\"root\" data-docx-has-block-data=\"false\">\n<div class=\"ace-line ace-line old-record-id-GAbtdVX4zo2Vmqxq6KZl9qG3gOf\">Looking at the two dominant producers, in 1985, the United States produced approximately 1200 megawatts of wind power, which was significantly higher than Denmark's figure of around 250 megawatts. The American output then rose markedly to reach nearly 1700 megawatts in 1990, before fluctuating slightly and ending at about 1500 megawatts in 2000. Meanwhile, Denmark showed steady growth throughout the period, eventually surpassing the United States to generate roughly 1650 megawatts by 2000.</div>\n</div>",
